(a) Purposes and intents. Experience shows when fire occurs in a building, the Fire Department is often faced with delayed entry into the structure. Entry into the structure, in some cases, must be made with force which is time consuming and demands extra energy by fire personnel. In order to expedite entry into a structure and to aid the Fire Department in the task of extinguishment, the following requirements have been developed. Likewise, police face delayed entry problem when regarding various emergency calls.
(b) Key boxes. Key boxes shall be required on all new and existing building sites (as provided for below) to allow the fire and police departments to enter a building quickly in case of an emergency. The key boxes shall be marked, conspicuous, and accessible. The key box to be installed in an approved location, shall be of an approved type listed in accordance with UL 1037. The fire officials shall approve the key boxes used.
(c) Requirements.
(1) All new occupancies built after November 1, 2021 and served by Rome Fire Department shall be provided with a key box, approved by the Rome Fire Department. A key box, approved by the Rome Fire Department shall be required to installed under the following circumstances:
a. All existing buildings with a change of ownership occurring after November 1, 2021;
b. Renovations or remodeling meeting the requirements of Chapter 43 of the Life Safety Code, NFPA 101, as described in the Rules and Regulations of the Georgia Safety Fire Commissioner, Ga. Comp. R. & Regs. R. 120-3-3.01 et seq.;
c. A third and any subsequent incident requiring Fire Department response that results in a false alarm; and
d. A refusal by a notified keyholder whether via E-911 or an alarm/monitoring company of a Fire Department response to a possible incident within a structure with a potential activated fire protection system or called in alarm.
Exception: Those facilities which provide 24-hour services, including, but not necessarily limited to, convenient stores, hospitals and 24-hour mercantile occupancies, etc.
(2) The key box shall be:
a. The Knox Box® or other similar key box approved by the Rome Fire Department;
b. Located at or near the recognized public entrance located as approved by the Rome Fire Department;
c. The key box shall be installed and operational prior to the issuance of an occupancy permit;
d. Located at a height of not more than five feet from the ground, sidewalk of paved surface near the primary or main entrance and directly adjacent to the wall, or other location designated by the fire marshal or fire code official.
(3) No steps, displays, signs or other fixtures, or structure protrusions shall be located under the key box which would allow intruders to access the box without assistance.
(4) Box contents: The key box shall contain the keys for the following. The keys shall be labeled so as to be easily identified by firefighters in the field.
a. Keys to locked points of egress, whether on the interior or exterior;
b. Keys to locked mechanical or electrical rooms;
c. Keys to the locked elevator or machine rooms;
d. Keys to the elevator(s);
e. Keys to any fence or secured gates;
f. Keys to any other areas as directed by the Fire Department.
(5) Key lock box for apartments.
a. All new apartment complexes which have limited access shall be required to install a key lock box.
1. Location will be determined by plans review or field;
2. The box shall be located at a height of not more than five feet from the ground, sidewalk of paved surface near the primary or main entrance and directly adjacent to the wall, or other location designated by the fire marshal or fire code official;
3. Key lockbox contents shall contain keys for the following (The keys shall be labeled to be easily identified in the field).
i. Alarm room area where the fire alarm panel is installed;
ii. Alarm panel;
iii. Any special keys or tools to reset pull stations;
iv. All other areas as specified by the Rome Fire Department.
b. Lock box need not be supervised.
c. Only apartment complex owners, management companies, or managers may purchase key lockboxes.
(6) The operator of the building shall immediately notify the fire code official and provide the new key where a lock is changed or rekeyed. The key to such lock shall be secured in the key box.
(7) Where access to or within a structure or an area is restricted because of secured openings or where immediate access is necessary for life-saving or firefighting purposes, the fire code official is authorized to require a key box to be installed in an approved location.
(8) Any person who owns or operates a structure subject to this section and fails to abide by the provisions hereof and violates any section, may be subject to a maximum fine for a conviction of such violation in an amount not to exceed $500.00.
(Ord. No. 2021-10-2, § I, 10-25-21)

